Yep, this is the practical network-investigation lab that connects DAIR scoping with Squid logs and tcpdump. The big story is: one CEO workstation alert expands into an almost environment-wide compromise through repeated pivots.

1. Starting point

Known initially:

CEO workstation: 172.16.42.107
Ransom note appeared around 1 PM
Ransom-note domain: midnitemeerkats.com
Therefore 172.16.42.107 is the initial known compromised host.

That gives you two excellent pivots:

HOST → 172.16.42.107
DOMAIN → midnitemeerkats.com


This is DAIR Scope in practice.

2. First pivot: search the Squid proxy

They search:

grep midnitemeerkats access.log


Why?

Because access.log contains the web requests observed by Squid.

It finds requests from:

172.16.42.107


to the ransom-note site.

So now an endpoint observation:

“Ransom note popped up around 1 PM”

has network evidence supporting it.

3. awk reduces the noise

The raw Squid entry has lots of fields. We only care about:

$1 → timestamp
$3 → requesting client
$7 → requested URL

So:

awk '/midnitemeerkats/ {print $1, $3, $7}' access.log


means:

For lines containing midnitemeerkats, print fields 1, 3 and 7.

Think of awk here almost like:

Select-Object


You're reducing the data to the fields you actually need.

4. Convert Epoch time

Squid's timestamp isn't convenient for a human.

So they use strftime() inside awk and set the timezone:

TZ=America/Los_Angeles awk \
'/midnitemeerkats/ {print strftime("%T",$1), $3, $7}' access.log


Now they can see activity around:

13:05:56
13:05:59
13:06:00


So the CEO saying “around 1 PM” correlates reasonably well with the recorded network activity around 1:05-1:06 PM.

Important IR lesson

Don't expect human recollection to give you second-level precision.

Use the human report as an anchor, then establish precise timing from evidence.

5. Now look for beaconing

This is where things become much more interesting.

Instead of only looking for the ransom-note site, the analysts ask:

Is the CEO machine repeatedly communicating somewhere?

The lab's findbeacons.py looks for repeated requests at roughly consistent intervals.

Conceptually:

13:00:00 → destination
13:00:05 → destination
13:00:10 → destination
13:00:15 → destination
...


That repeating pattern can indicate beacon-like activity.

Again:

Beacon-like ≠ automatically C2.

But it's an excellent investigation lead.

6. Suspicious lookalike domain

The lab finds traffic to something resembling:

www1-google-analytics.com


The important lesson isn't memorizing the domain.

It's recognizing lookalike infrastructure.

At a quick glance:

www1-google-analytics.com


might look like something associated with legitimate Google Analytics.

But analysts should validate the actual domain rather than mentally interpreting familiar words inside it.

This connects perfectly to your earlier question about something that looks like a Microsoft domain.

Golden rule:

Trust the actual domain/ownership/context, not how legitimate the hostname looks at first glance.

7. Now PIVOT on the suspected C2 indicator ⭐

This is the most important part.

You've discovered a new indicator.

DAIR says:

🔄 GO BACK TO SCOPE

They search Squid logs for other clients communicating with the suspicious domain:

awk '/www1-google-analytics.com/ {print $3}' access.log | sort -u


Breakdown:

awk
 ↓
find matching requests

print $3
 ↓
extract requesting IP

sort -u
 ↓
return unique IP addresses


This produces multiple internal hosts.

So:

CEO
 ↓
Suspicious beacon
 ↓
C2/domain IOC
 ↓
Search environment
 ↓
Additional hosts


🔥 That's textbook scoping.

8. But the proxy has a blind spot

Remember from the previous module?

Squid logs aren't necessarily showing all network traffic.

The material specifically warns that HTTP/HTTPS on non-standard ports may not appear in the expected proxy evidence.

So if you only search access.log, you may underestimate the incident.

What do they do?

Pivot to the PCAP.

This is an important IR concept:

When one data source reaches its visibility limit, pivot to another evidence source.

9. Domain → IP

First they need the IP associated with their IOC.

They query existing evidence:

grep www1-google-analytics.com access.log | head -n 1


head -n 1 means:

Give me the first result only.

From the lab evidence they obtain:

167.172.201.123


Now they've transformed:

DOMAIN IOC
www1-google-analytics.com


into:

IP IOC
167.172.201.123


Another pivot.

10. Search PCAP using tcpdump

Now:

tcpdump -nr falsimentis.pcap 'dst host 167.172.201.123'


Translation:

-n
Don't resolve names

-r falsimentis.pcap
Read this PCAP

dst host 167.172.201.123
Show packets whose destination is that IP


This reveals additional internal systems that weren't all apparent from the proxy investigation.

That is precisely why SEC504 said:

Don't rely upon one evidence source.

11. The ugly cut command 😄

The lab then does something like:

tcpdump -nr falsimentis.pcap 'dst host 167.172.201.123' |
cut -d ' ' -f 3 |
cut -d '.' -f 1-4 |
sort -u


Don't memorize this whole string blindly.

Understand the workflow:

tcpdump

Get packets going to suspicious IP.

↓

First cut

Extract the source-address field from tcpdump's text output.

↓

Second cut

Strip off the source port, leaving the IP.

Think:

172.16.42.107.50453
        ↓
172.16.42.107


↓

sort -u

Give me only unique hosts.

So effectively the command answers:

Which distinct machines communicated with this suspicious destination?

12. Scope massively expanded

The investigation started with:

172.16.42.107
CEO


But IOC pivoting finds other systems contacting the identified infrastructure, including systems such as:

172.16.42.2
172.16.42.3
172.16.42.103
172.16.42.105
172.16.42.107
172.16.42.108
172.16.42.109


This is the exact lesson from Argous again.

Initial impression

One employee's workstation has ransomware.

After scoping

The incident appears substantially broader.

13. Find when activity started

Now that we know WHO, the next question is:

WHEN?

They use tcpdump -c 1.

This part matters:

-c 1


means:

Stop after the first matching packet.

Therefore for each compromised system:

Host → suspicious destination


they retrieve the first observed matching packet.

This helps construct an incident timeline.

14. The for loop looks horrible, but conceptually it's easy 😂

You see:

for octet in 2 3 103 105 107 108 109; do
   ...
done


It's basically saying:

Run the same tcpdump search for each suspected internal host.

Instead of manually typing:

172.16.42.2
172.16.42.3
172.16.42.103
172.16.42.105
...


the loop changes $octet each time:

172.16.42.$octet


Simple automation.

15. Here's an important finding: port 8090

The initial matching packets are communicating to:

167.172.201.123:8090


Notice:

8090

not traditional HTTP 80 or HTTPS 443.

🔥 This explains why relying solely on the proxy evidence could miss useful network activity.

So now the lesson becomes extremely clear:

Proxy logs
     ↓
valuable but incomplete

PCAP
     ↓
reveals additional traffic

16. Then specifically investigate port 80

They modify the BPF expression by adding:

and dst port 80


So now:

src host X
AND
dst host 167.172.201.123
AND
dst port 80


This is a great BPF example.

Break it down:

src host
→ Who sent it?

dst host
→ Where did it go?

dst port
→ Which destination service/port?


All conditions must match because they're joined with:

and

