↓

BUILD TIMELINE

That's threat hunting + incident scoping.

17. Then they build an SE3R timeline

Don't get too hung up on the visual.

The important concept is:

Event line

Represents:

What happened when?

For example:

09:10
Host A activity

09:38
Host B activity

...

13:05
CEO ransom note

Knowledge bins

Group related contextual information.

Such as:

Host
IP
Role
Indicator
Relevant evidence


The advantage is that you're no longer looking at disconnected facts.

You're reconstructing:

The incident as a sequence of related events.

🎯 Commands from this lab worth knowing
Find a string
grep midnitemeerkats access.log


Search log lines.

Extract fields
awk '/midnitemeerkats/ {print $1, $3, $7}' access.log


Think:

$1 = timestamp
$3 = client
$7 = URL


for this Squid log format.

Unique values
sort -u


Sort + unique results.

First result
head -n 1


First line.

PCAP destination search
tcpdump -nr falsimentis.pcap 'dst host 167.172.201.123'


Traffic destined for IOC IP.

First matching packet
tcpdump -nr falsimentis.pcap -c 1 '...filter...'


Stop after first match.

Add port constraint
and dst port 80


Destination must also be TCP/UDP port 80 as applicable to the filter context.

🔥 Most important GCIH lesson from this lab

Don't memorize the fictional IP addresses.

Memorize the investigation logic:

Known compromised host → identify related indicator → pivot on indicator → discover additional hosts → pivot to another evidence source → establish scope → extract timestamps → construct timeline.

And notice how beautifully this fits DAIR:

DETECT
CEO ransom note
     ↓
VERIFY
Network evidence confirms relevant activity
     ↓
SCOPE
Known host → IOC → other hosts
     ↓
NEW IOC FOUND
     ↓
SCOPE AGAIN 🔄


The Argous scenario taught you why poor scoping is dangerous.

This Falsimentis lab is now teaching you how to perform that scoping using real evidence.
